    words_note='Before the first idea, five words to carry through the course. A model is the thing that has learned patterns. The parameters are its weights and biases, the numbers inside it. Training adjusts those parameters by repeatedly measuring a loss. Inference is using the trained model. And overfitting is when a model fits its training data, noise included, and does worse on new data. We meet every one of these again, starting with why this session matters to you.',
    word_icons=['robot', 'switch', 'book', 'speech', 'cross'],
    cover_note='Welcome, and thank you for being here. This is the first session of the course, so there is no earlier session to build on. Before we build anything, we spend it on the ideas that sit under everything else: how AI and machine learning actually work, what the fields and jobs are, and why a company leader needs this picture. There are no exercises today. You leave with a handbook to read before Session 1.',
    outcome_note='That question gives us three things to aim for. A clear picture of how AI works, how a model is measured and what happens behind a chat answer. A map of the fields, the jobs, the data and the costs behind AI work in a company. And your handbook, to read before Session 1. Nothing to submit and nothing to be tested on.',
    keep_note='That brings us back to the question we began with, so before you go, here is what to take with you. The handbook repeats today’s ideas with pictures and has a glossary you can search. Reading it is the only preparation for Session 1, which begins with how a machine sees. Bring a laptop and an AI assistant account you already use, because some demonstrations let you join in. If anything today was unclear, write the question down. We start Session 1 by answering it.',
